==English==

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|eke|t=(''obsolete'') to add to, augment; to increase}} + {{m|en|out}}.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[supplement]].
#: {{ux|en|The old man '''eked out''' his pension by selling vegetables from his garden.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Houghton (apothecary)|John Houghton]], compiler|chapter=A Letter from a ''Lancashire'' Friend about Breeding Cattle.{{nb...|They Teach Them to Drink, and Feed Them with Pottage and Whey. Great Stores Bred in This County.}}|editor=[[w:Richard Bradley (botanist)|Richard Bradley]]|title=Husbandry and Trade Improv’d: Being a Collection of Many Valuable Materials Relating to Corn, Cattle, Coals, Hops, Wool, &c. {{...|With a Compleat Catalogue of the Several Sorts of Earths, and Their Proper Product; the Best Sorts of Manure for Each; with the Art of Draining and Flooding of Lands; as also Full and Exact Histories of Trades, as Malting, Brewing, &c. the Description and Structure of Instruments for Husbandry, and Carriages, with the Manner of Their Improvement; an Account of the Rivers of England, &c. and how far They may be Made Navigable; of Weight and Measures, of Woods, Cordage, and Metals; of Building and Stowage, the Vegetation of Plants, &c. with Many Other Useful Particulars Communicated by Several Eminent Members of the Royal Society, to the Collector, John Houghton, F.R.S. Now Revised, Corrected, and Published, with a Preface and Useful Indexes}} In Three Volumes|location=London|publisher=Prin[t]ed for Woo[d]man and Lyon{{nb...|in Russel-street, Covent Garden.}}|date={{J2G|28 September 1694}}|year_published=1727|issue=CXIII|page=303|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wERRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA303|oclc=911709521|passage=Now the reaſons why they teach the calves to drink ſo ſoon are various. [...] ''Secondly'', the goodwife ſaves milk by this way of drinking, for ſhe quickly '''ekes out''' the milk with pottage, &c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Art. XIV. ''History of Great Britain.'' By William Belsham. Vol. XI. and XII. London, 1805. 8vo. [book review]|magazine=[[w:Edinburgh Review|The Edinburgh Review, or Critical Journal]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed by D. Willison,{{nb...|Craigs Close}}, for [[w:Constable & Robinson|Archibald Constable & Co.]]{{nb...|Edinburgh}}, and [[w:Longman|Longman Hurst Rees and Orme]],{{nb...|London.}}|month=July|year=1805|volume=VI|issue=XII|page=428|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rEc7AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA428|oclc=950902861|passage=[T]he author &#91;{{w|William Belsham}}&#93; '''ekes out''' his volume with a great many extraneous details, which relate to a ſubſequent period; [...] The whole work is ſingularly confuſed and deſultory: and, indeed, the plan which the author adopts, is altogether incompatible with that unity and coherence which is eſſential to hiſtory.}}
#* {{RQ:Mill Political Economy|chapter=Continuation of the Same Subject [Of Peasant Proprietors]|volume=I|section=4|page=338|passage=A majority of the properties are so small as not to afford a subsistence to the proprietors, of whom, according to some computations, as many as three millions are obliged to '''eke out''' their means of support either by working for hire, or by taking additional land, generally on metayer tenure.}}
#* {{RQ:Graves Claudius|chapter=I|page=3|passage=[I]t is indeed Claudius himself who is writing this book, and no mere secretary of his, and not one of those official annalists, either, to whom public men are in the habit of communicating their recollections, in the hope that elegant writing will '''eke out''' meagreness of subject-matter and flattery soften vices.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[obtain]] with [[difficulty]] or [[effort]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''eked out''' a living selling vegetables from the garden.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kamin Mohammadi|authorlink=Kamin Mohammadi|chapter=Displaced|title=The Cypress Tree: A Love Letter to Iran|location=London|publisher={{w|Bloomsbury Publishing}}|year=2011|isbn=978-0-7475-9152-8|edition2=paperback|location2=London|publisher2=Bloomsbury Publishing|year2=2012|page2=197|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=L_RqWaBZqLcC&pg=PA197|isbn2=978-1-4088-2233-3|passage=But before too long, the rations that Parivash was '''ekeing out''' to feed them fell short and the tension that sprang from so many families piled in together overflowed.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Ben Perry|title=Branson’s spaceship steals the spotlight at airshow|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200426193640/https://sg.news.yahoo.com/bransons-spaceship-puts-subdued-plane-giants-shade-170104236.html|archivedate=26 April 2020|work={{w|Yahoo! News}}|date=12 July 2012|passage=British tycoon {{w|Richard Branson}} stole the show here Wednesday, announcing that he and his family would be on Virgin Galactic's first trip into space, as Airbus and Boeing '''eked out''' more plane orders.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Meriel Fuller|chapter=1|title=The Knight’s Fugitive Lady|series=Harlequin Historical|seriesvolume=370|location=Don Mills, Ont.|publisher={{w|Harlequin Enterprises}}|year=2013|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_U43AAAAQBAJ&pg=PA7|isbn=978-0-373-30680-0|passage=Her stomach growled at the prospect of eating roast rabbit for breakfast; the last time she had eaten meat had been three days ago. Since then, they had been '''ekeing out''' the last dusty contents of a sack of oats, watered down and cooked to make a sloppy gruel.}}

